PART 10: TRANSITIONING TO REAL DATA
================================================================================

CURRENT APPROACH
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Fake data:

FAKE_GROCERIES
       ↓
useState()
       ↓
UI


Example:

const [
  groceryItems,
  setGroceryItems
] = useState(
  FAKE_GROCERIES
)


================================================================================
FUTURE API SHAPE - CONCEPTUAL EXAMPLE
================================================================================

If the application later uses an HTTP API, it may need state such as:

const [
  groceryItems,
  setGroceryItems
] = useState([])


const [loading, setLoading] =
  useState(true)


const [error, setError] =
  useState(null)


Then the application would:

1. Request grocery records

2. Check the HTTP response

3. Convert the response body

4. Store successful data

5. Store/report failures

6. Stop the loading state


Conceptually:

useEffect(() => {

  const fetchGroceries =
    async () => {

      try {

        setLoading(true)

        setError(null)


        const response =
          await fetch(
            '/api/groceries'
          )


        if (!response.ok) {

          throw new Error(
            `Request failed: ${response.status}`
          )
        }


        const data =
          await response.json()


        setGroceryItems(
          data
        )

      } catch (error) {

        setError(
          error instanceof Error
            ? error.message
            : 'Unable to load groceries'
        )

      } finally {

        setLoading(false)
      }
    }


  fetchGroceries()

}, [])


================================================================================
IMPORTANT API TRANSITION NOTE
================================================================================

The statement:

"Only replace fake data and everything else remains unchanged"

is too simplistic.

A real API can introduce additional concerns:

• Loading state

• Error state

• HTTP status handling

• Async add/update/delete operations

• Server-generated IDs

• Data-shape validation

• Authentication/authorization

• Retry behavior

• Request cancellation

• Optimistic vs confirmed UI updates


Therefore, much of the PRESENTATION logic may remain similar, but the data
management layer can become significantly more complex.


================================================================================
FAKE DATA VS API DATA
================================================================================

+-----------------------+-------------------------+----------------------------+
| AREA                  | FAKE DATA               | API DATA                   |
+-----------------------+-------------------------+----------------------------+
| Initial Source        | Imported sample array   | HTTP/backend response      |
| Async Loading         | Usually no              | Usually yes                |
| Failure State         | Usually unnecessary     | Required consideration     |
| IDs                   | Local fixture values    | Often server-provided      |
| Mutations             | Local setState          | May require HTTP request   |
| Data Validation       | Structurally known      | Validate contract          |
| Authentication        | Not involved            | May be involved            |
| Persistence           | No                      | Depends on backend         |
+-----------------------+-------------------------+----------------------------+


================================================================================
KEEP UI LOGIC SEPARATE FROM DATA SOURCE
================================================================================

A useful design goal is:

DATA SOURCE
   ↓
Normalized Grocery Items
   ↓
Presentation Components


Then the list-rendering code can operate on a predictable item structure
whether data originated from:

• Fake fixtures

• localStorage

• API

• Database-backed service


